---
const features = [
  {
    title: "Typed entries, checked on every write",
    description:
      "Define person, decision, event or component types in <code>kb.yaml</code>. Every write is validated against the schema, whether a person or an agent made it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Every change is a commit",
    description:
      "Markdown with YAML frontmatter, versioned in git. Branch, diff, review and roll back what you know the same way you do code.",
  },
  {
    title: "Search by meaning and by structure",
    description:
      "Vector search finds what an entry is about. Structured queries find it by type, tag, date range or relationship. Use both at once.",
  },
  {
    title: "Yours, on your disk",
    description:
      "Plain files plus a SQLite index you can rebuild at any time. No cloud dependency, no subscription, nothing to export your way out of.",
  },
];
---

<section id="why" aria-labelledby="why-title" class="home-section flex flex-col gap-12">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4 max-w-[44rem]">
    <h2 id="why-title" class="section-title">Built for knowledge that has to hold up</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-secondary text-pretty">
      Most AI memory is a pile of embeddings. Most wikis are a pile of pages.
      Pyrite treats knowledge the way you already treat code: typed, reviewed,
      versioned, and readable by machines and people alike.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(250px,100%),1fr))] gap-x-8 gap-y-10">
    {
      features.map((f, i) => (
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2.5 pt-5 border-t border-line">
          <span class="font-mono text-xs text-brand">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold leading-[1.4]">{f.title}</h3>
          <p class="text-text-secondary [&_code]:font-mono [&_code]:text-[13px] [&_code]:text-text-primary" set:html={f.description} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>
